WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.320
Humanoid robots promise to move through the same constrained
environments as humans, but

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.380
sparse footholds remain a major challenge.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.660
On stepping stones, a small foot placement error can cause
complete failure.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.740
In this work, we present MARCH, a model-assisted reinforcement
learning framework for perceptive

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.080
humanoid locomotion over sparse footholds.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.180
In our training, we take advantage of a teacher-student framework
where the teacher has access to

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:32.980
privileged information, which is then distilled into a student
with noisy perception and proprioception.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.940
We first generate reference trajectories using simplified models,
including planned footholds

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.420
and a center-of-mass reference from a hybrid linear inverted
pendulum model.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:48.020
The teacher policy is trained in simulation to track these
references using control Lyapunov-inspired

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.020
rewards.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.140
Then, this behavior is distilled into a student policy that only
receives deployable observations,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.420
depth images, and proprioceptive history.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.540
A central challenge in this distillation step is that the
teacher's behavior is not

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.460
always unimodal.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.980
From the same robot state, multiple stepping choices may be
valid.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:14.060
For example, the robot may be able to step with either foot, or
choose between safe footholds

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.460
on the left or right side of an obstacle.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.300
A standard unimodal student can average these valid actions and
produce an invalid one,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.380
such as placing the foot between two stones.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.980
To address this, the student uses a mixture density network,
which represents multiple

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.180
possible action modes instead of collapsing them into a single
mean.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:39.380
We compare the model-assisted distilled policy against a model-
free distilled policy.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.980
Both policies attempt the same sparse foothold task, but their
motion quality is different.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.500
The model-free policy tends to rely on more local corrections,
while the model-assisted

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.380
policy produces smoother transitions between supports and more
consistent foot placement.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.260
This qualitative difference is also reflected in the metrics.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:05.580
Relative to the model-free baseline, our policy reduces mean
joint torque by at least 12%,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:12.140
and reduces angular jerk by 39%, while maintaining similar
results in traversing the path.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.220
One interesting behavior apparent from our trials is presented
here.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.940
Before committing to the forward foothold, the robot first takes
a backward step.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.620
That happens because the robot learned from its model that it
does not have enough momentum

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.260
to reach the next step.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.740
That's why it steps back to gain more momentum to be able to
reach the next stepping stone.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.980
Finally, we return to the hardware result.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:39.340
The deployed student policy does not receive privileged terrain
information, teacher observations,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.060
or model-generated references.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.660
It runs on board from depth and proprioceptive history.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.460
Overall, MARCH shows that model-guided reinforcement learning can
improve perceptive humanoid locomotion

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.460
while remaining transferable to hardware.
